      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town officially became incorporated in 1916, marking its first formal step toward becoming a community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Braselton%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1989, actress Kim Basinger and investors bought 1,751 acres of Braselton for $20 million, hoping to turn it into a tourist hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Braselton%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Braselton‑Stover House, built in 1918 by Green Braselton, now hosts weddings and receptions in a historic setting.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.georgiaencyclopedia.org/articles/counties-cities-neighborhoods/braselton/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Braselton stretches across four counties—Barrow, Gwinnett, Hall, and Jackson—making it a unique cross‑county town.",
